Each revision of a series has opened a thread of its own, so the
versions of one series and their review are spread across the list.
This series posts a revision as a reply to the first version's cover
letter, or its only patch, so every version stays in one thread. That
is the shape git.git has recommended since June 2026, as "Re: [PATCH
1/3] libexec/mail: Thread each revision under its first version" [1]
lays out.

Patch 1 makes format-patch.sh pick the reply target. It already has
v(N-1) from --previous, so it follows In-Reply-To up from there to
v1. A revision posted in a thread of its own, under the old
convention, ends the walk, so its next version joins that thread.
Patch 2 brings lab-process-mail and the mail workflow guide in line,
and patch 3 does the same for freebsd-port, which formats ports with
plain git format-patch. The Link to v(N-1) line stays for readers.

The series needs "[PATCH v2 0/4] Require an interdiff on revised
series" [2], which adds --previous; its patch ids are in the
prerequisite-patch-id lines below. With it, the series applies to
b/dev@upstream. It cannot apply to bare trunk: trunk has neither
--previous nor the freebsd-port skill that patch 3 edits.

I tested the walk in patch 1 against the list. From the v2 cover, or
from one of its patches, it reaches the v1 cover; from the v1 cover it
stays there; an unknown Message-ID stops it with an error. This v3 is
threaded under v1 with the same code.

